Linux-port HEVC speed (development record #379): soc: rockchip: RK3128 DDR3 frequency change.

The loader trains the DDR3 at 300 MHz and mainline has no driver that changes it, while the
Rockchip 3.10 kernel raises it to 396 MHz at boot (ddrfreq "normal 396MHz", vdd_logic
1.25 V); the HEVC decoder is DDR-bound, so mainline decodes slower than Android. This ports
the vendor's arch/arm/mach-rockchip/ddr_rk3126.c (GPL-2.0, rockchip-linux/kernel
release-3.10, "version 1.02 20140828" plus its later PHY de-reset and clock-gate fixes),
DDR3 only: the same timings (speed bin DDR3_DEFAULT) and the same self-refresh, DPLL and
uPCTL sequence, run from the internal SRAM with the other CPUs parked there and the NoC
ports idled. At probe it goes to the node's rockchip,boot-mhz (rk3128_dmc.boot_mhz
overrides it; 0 or absent leaves the loader's rate); a write to
/sys/kernel/debug/rk3128-dmc/mhz raises it at run time. Only up, to 301-400 MHz: the
vendor's settings for 300 MHz and below are its DRAM-DLL-off mode, and a switch back down
to them decoded with errors on the board (attempt 379-7). The logic rail is required and
goes to 1.25 V first; without it nothing changes. It acts only on the DDR3 controller
state it knows.

Hardened in development record #387: the SRAM functions are built without tracing,
sanitizers, coverage or loop-to-memcpy conversion (and scripts/linux-build.sh fails the build
on any relocation or indirect call in them); the NoC idle handshake gives up after 10 ms,
releases the requests and changes nothing (the vendor waits forever); debugfs rk3128-dmc/regs
dumps the registers the switch writes; rk3128_dmc.pd_idle and rk3128_dmc.trfc_ns are test
knobs (the vendor's 0x40 and its die-size tRFC by default; tRFC only longer).

Reworked in development record #456 after a boot hung at the switch (attempt 428-1: the
last line "300 -> 396 MHz", CPU 3 deaf to the backtrace IPI, the RCU GP kthread starved on
CPU 3, a watchdog reset): every switch runs on CPU 0 in a kworker bound there, as the
vendor's ddrfreqd (bound to CPU 0) does, so the rail's ramp and settle sleeps never wake on
another CPU's run queue. The other CPUs are stopped as stop_machine() does, through a work
on each CPU's stopper thread (a scheduling point: no spinlock held, no driver mid-sequence),
which reports in with interrupts on, then on "go" turns them off, cleans its L1 and spins
in SRAM; instead of the #379 asynchronous IPI that froze each CPU wherever it was. Every
phase has a deadline (report in 100 ms, park 10 ms, back out 100 ms) and a miss names the
CPUs and changes nothing, or reports the switch failed; the release wait is no longer
ignored, and a NoC de-idle timeout is reported. One line ("vdd_logic ... switching on CPU
0") marks the step before the CPUs stop. debugfs rk3128-dmc/test is the hardware test kit:
"park N", "relock N" (the whole sequence at the current rate, 1 MiB read back) and
"ipipark N" (the #379 park, for the A/B), built only with CONFIG_ROCKCHIP_RK3128_DMC_TEST
(the hevc test image): a product build has neither the kit nor the #379 park.

The kit's first frame runs (attempts 456-1, 456-2) missed about one park in 400 and one relock
in 90, always on CPUs that, by their own stamps, parked within 10 us: CPU 0 waited for the park
with its interrupts on and read the clock after scanning the flags, and every device interrupt
of this board lands on CPU 0 (the eMMC's PIO data moves in its interrupt handler), so an
interrupt between a scan and the clock read that kept CPU 0 away past the 10 ms deadline
called a miss on CPUs long parked. Now CPU 0 turns its interrupts off at "go" (as
multi_cpu_stop() does on every CPU in one step), every deadline reads the clock before it
scans, and each CPU stamps its phases (reported in, saw "go", interrupts off, parked, released,
its longest time away while waiting) into its SRAM slot; a miss logs all of them, with CPU 0's
interrupts in test builds. The kit gains "irqpark N" (the 15db184 park, for this A/B) and goes
on past a park miss, which changes nothing. The stamps read the physical count (CNTPCT): the
virtual one carries a per-CPU offset nothing programs here. Attempts 456-3 and 456-4: 0 misses
in 6000 parks and 600 relocks, against 9 and 5 in 3000 for the 15db184 park in the same boots.

